## The development of supply and sales on the Bratislava new-build market

Supply grew and sales grew with it. Over the last {qDays} days {qSalesNow} flats were sold against {qSalesThen} in the preceding {qDays} days, that is {qSalesChangePctEn}% more. Rising supply is therefore not yet accompanied by slowing sales.

The average achieved price of sold flats reached {qSoldM2} €/m² incl. VAT, practically matching the average asking price of {qMeanM2} €/m² incl. VAT. The average absolute price of sold flats was {qSoldPrice} € incl. VAT at an average floor area of {qSoldAreaEn} m².

## A view on where the market is heading

Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata: "The Bratislava market is in a state where supply and sales are both rising and prices are standing still. On a comparable sample of projects the average price moved by {qPanelM2ChangePctEn}% over the last two months, which in practice is zero. So with supply rising, developers are neither cutting prices nor raising them.

The average achieved price has matched the asking price, which means that what sells is not markedly cheaper than what is on offer. At the same time the average floor area of sold flats is rising, so buyers have lately been going for larger flats.

Unless interest rates and household purchasing power change materially, we expect this state to continue: supply will keep growing and prices will stay roughly where they are. What will be worth watching is whether rising supply eventually starts to push prices down."

**Author:** Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata · tomas@residata.eu
